# Chapter 5 · Women and Employment: Participation, Barriers and the Double Burden

> "Women's participation in the labour force has increased recently, yet women continue to perform their **reproductive roles**. Discuss the **challenges** encountered by women as a result of paid employment worldwide, with special reference to Pakistan" (2019). MCQs asked about the **glass ceiling** (2024), the **sticky floor** (2026) and **comparable worth** (2023).

## 5.1  Women's work in Pakistan — the data

::: data Women in Pakistan's labour force | Labour Force Survey 2024–25 (PBS)
- Female labour-force participation: ==24.4%== (up from about 21.4% in 2020–21).
- By province: **Punjab** 33.8%, **Khyber Pakhtunkhwa** 21.1%, **Sindh** 20.5%, **Balochistan** 14.5%.
- Most employed women work in **agriculture** or in **informal** work — much of it unpaid family work or home-based piece work.
- Pakistan ranked ==144th of 145== on **economic participation** in the Global Gender Gap Report 2026.
:::

::: fig bars | Female labour-force participation by province, 2024–25 (%).
@unit %
@colour 2F7D4F
Punjab: 33.8
Khyber Pakhtunkhwa: 21.1
Sindh: 20.5
Balochistan: 14.5
Pakistan: 24.4 *
:::

## 5.2  Where women work

1. **Agriculture** — sowing, weeding, cotton picking, harvesting and livestock care; often unpaid, as family helpers.
2. **Home-based work** — stitching, embroidery, bangle-making, packing, often for piece rates through middlemen.
3. **Domestic work** — cooking and cleaning in other people's homes, without contracts.
4. **Services** — teaching and health (teachers, nurses, Lady Health Workers).
5. **Manufacturing** — garments, textiles, pharmaceuticals, food processing.
6. **Professions and new sectors** — doctors, lawyers, bankers, IT, freelancing; small but growing.

## 5.3  The double burden: paid work plus reproductive roles

::: define Reproductive roles | the 2019 question's key phrase
The unpaid work of **bearing and raising children** and **maintaining the household** — cooking, cleaning, caring for the sick and elderly. Caroline Moser calls this women's **reproductive** role, alongside their **productive** (paid) and **community** roles (the **triple role**).
:::

When women take paid jobs, their reproductive work rarely falls. They work a "**second shift**" at home (**Arlie Hochschild**, ==1989==). The result is the **double burden**: long hours, fatigue, stress and little rest or leisure.

## 5.4  Challenges of paid employment — worldwide

::: fig cycle | The challenges women face in paid employment.
@centre Challenges of paid work
Double burden of paid and unpaid work
Unequal pay and "women's jobs"
Glass ceiling and sticky floor
Harassment at work and on the way
Lack of childcare and maternity protection
Insecure and informal work
Social disapproval and guilt
:::

1. **The double burden** — paid work plus unpaid care; women globally do about three times as much unpaid care work as men (ILO, ==2018==).
2. **Unequal pay** — the **gender pay gap**; women's jobs are valued less. **Comparable worth** means equal pay for work of **equal value** — its absence leads to "sex segregation and less pay for women's work" (MCQ 2023).
3. **Occupational segregation** — women crowded into teaching, nursing, garments and care.
4. **The glass ceiling** — invisible barriers to the top (MCQ 2024); few women managers or board members.
5. **The sticky floor** — women stuck in **low-wage, low-mobility** jobs (MCQ 2026).
6. **The motherhood penalty** — lower pay and promotion after having children.
7. **Harassment** — at work and while commuting.
8. **Lack of childcare and maternity protection**.
9. **Insecure, informal work** — no contracts, pensions or social security.
10. **Social disapproval** — criticism for "neglecting" family; guilt; conflict at home.

## 5.5  The challenges in Pakistan

### (1) Mobility and safety

Unsafe, crowded **public transport** and **harassment** on the street keep many women out of work. Families often forbid work far from home. Some cities have introduced **women-only buses** and ride-hailing, with mixed success.

### (2) Social norms

The ideal of the **male breadwinner** and family **honour** discourage women's work outside the home, especially in mixed workplaces. Many families accept **teaching and medicine** but not other jobs.

### (3) The double burden in joint families

Working women in joint families may still be expected to do most cooking and care, and to defer to in-laws.

### (4) Pay and informality

Most working women are in **informal** jobs without contracts. The ILO has reported one of the **widest gender pay gaps** in the world in Pakistan. Home-based workers earn very low piece rates.

### (5) Harassment at work

The **Protection against Harassment of Women at the Workplace Act** was passed in ==2010== (MCQs 2016, 2018, 2019, 2024) and **amended in 2022** to widen the definitions of "workplace" and "employee". It requires inquiry committees and created an **ombudsperson**; enforcement is uneven, especially in the private and informal sectors.

### (6) Childcare and maternity

Few workplaces have **day-care centres**. Laws have improved: the federal **Maternity and Paternity Leave Act 2020** (Islamabad) extended paid maternity leave and introduced paternity leave; **Sindh** passed a Maternity Benefits Act in ==2018==. Coverage in the private and informal sectors is weak.

### (7) The "missing" educated women

Many educated women — including large numbers of **medical graduates** — do not enter or leave the workforce after marriage.

### (8) Quotas and public jobs

A **10% quota** for women in federal services was introduced in the 2000s (MCQ 2018 key: under the PML-Q government, 2002); provinces have their own quotas. Many quota posts go unfilled.

## 5.5a  Women in agriculture

Agriculture employs most working women in Pakistan. Women sow, transplant rice, weed, pick cotton, harvest wheat, cut fodder and care for livestock, as well as processing crops at home. Much of this work is **unpaid family labour** or paid at very low seasonal rates, often to the husband rather than the woman. Women rarely **own land** or control its income, and seldom receive credit, extension advice or new technology. Cotton pickers work long hours in heat, exposed to pesticides. Recognising women as **farmers** — through land rights, extension services for women, and minimum wages for farm labour — would raise both their status and agricultural output.

## 5.5b  Home-based workers

Millions of Pakistani women work at home — stitching garments, embroidering, making bangles, rolling incense sticks, packing goods — for **piece rates** paid through **middlemen**. Because they work at home, their work is often counted as "housework" and escapes labour laws. They have no contracts, minimum wage, social security or pensions, and they carry the costs of electricity and equipment. The **Sindh Home-Based Workers Act 2018** was the first law to recognise them as workers with rights to minimum wages and social security; other provinces have moved more slowly. Organisations such as the **Home-Based Women Workers Federation** have campaigned for these rights.

## 5.5c  Women in the formal sector and professions

A small but growing number of Pakistani women work in the **formal** economy — in banks, telecoms, pharmaceuticals, universities, the civil service, the armed forces, the police and the courts. Women have become fighter pilots, generals, judges of the Supreme Court (the first woman judge of the Supreme Court, **Justice Ayesha Malik**, took oath in ==2022==) and chief executives. These role models matter, but they remain exceptions: women hold a very small share of managerial and senior posts, and the professions they enter are often segregated.

## 5.6  Benefits of paid work — the other side

Paid work brings women **income, independence, confidence, social networks** and a stronger voice at home. Studies of garment workers in Bangladesh (**Naila Kabeer**, ==2000==) found that wage work delayed marriage and increased women's bargaining power. The challenge is to make work **decent**, not to keep women out of it.

## 5.7  Laws and programmes

::: table | Laws and programmes on women's work in Pakistan.
@widths 1.6,5.4,7
Year | Law or programme | Effect
2000s | 10% quota in federal services | Reserved posts for women
==2010== | Protection against Harassment of Women at the Workplace Act (amended 2022) | Inquiry committees; ombudsperson
==2018== | Sindh Home-Based Workers Act | First recognition of home-based workers
==2018== | Sindh Maternity Benefits Act | Maternity leave and benefits
==2019== | Punjab Domestic Workers Act | Rights for domestic workers
==2020== | Maternity and Paternity Leave Act (Islamabad) | Longer paid maternity leave; paternity leave
==2008== onward | BISP cash to women | Income in women's own names
:::

## 5.8  The way forward

1. **Safe transport** and enforcement of the **harassment law** on the street and at work.
2. **Childcare** — workplace day-care, public crèches, school hours that fit working hours.
3. **Share care** — paternity leave; campaigns for men's share of housework.
4. **Equal pay** for work of equal value; minimum wages enforced for women.
5. **Protect informal workers** — home-based and domestic workers' laws in all provinces; social security.
6. **Skills and digital work** — vocational and digital training; safe online work.
7. **Women in management** — mentoring, targets for boards and senior posts.
8. **Count unpaid work** — time-use surveys; recognise care in policy (**SDG target 5.4**).

::: eye | The model structure for 2019
1. Introduction: participation rising (24.4%), yet reproductive roles remain — the double burden. 2. Reproductive roles and the triple role (Moser). 3. Worldwide challenges (ten, with the MCQ terms glass ceiling, sticky floor, comparable worth). 4. Pakistan's challenges (mobility, norms, joint families, pay and informality, harassment, childcare). 5. The benefits of paid work. 6. Laws and programmes (table). 7. Way forward. 8. Conclusion: make work decent and share care. Draw the cycle figure.
:::
